WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:02.670
ALEXIS MOUSSINE-POUCHKINE:
Containers have drastically

00:00:02.670 --> 00:00:04.440
changed the workflow
of many developers.

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:07.200
And so you might wonder,
how do I run containers

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:08.970
on Google Cloud Platform?

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:10.770
In this video, we'll
review three ways you

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:12.390
can run your containers on GCP.

00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:15.240
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:18.925 --> 00:00:21.300
While the concepts underlying
containers have been around

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:24.030
for many years,
Docker, Kubernetes,

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:27.090
and an entire ecosystem of
products and best practices

00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:28.980
have emerged in
the last few years

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:31.140
enabling many different
kinds of applications

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:32.189
to be containerized.

00:00:32.189 --> 00:00:34.830
As a developer, containers
give you a lot of freedom

00:00:34.830 --> 00:00:38.070
by letting you package an app
with all of its dependencies

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:39.780
into an easy-to-move package.

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:42.120
The solutions for
running containers in GCP

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:45.060
vary essentially in how much of
the underlying infrastructure

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:45.990
is exposed.

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:48.450
The first way you can
run a container on GCP

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:52.110
is to use Google
Kubernetes Engine, or GKE.

00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:54.330
As the inventor of
Kubernetes, Google quite

00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:57.390
naturally offers a fully
managed Kubernetes service,

00:00:57.390 --> 00:01:00.240
taking care of scheduling
and scaling your containers

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:02.310
while monitoring their
health and state.

00:01:02.310 --> 00:01:04.769
Getting your code
to production on GKE

00:01:04.769 --> 00:01:06.600
can be as simple as
creating a container

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:09.870
deployment with the cluster
being provisioned on the fly.

00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:14.070
Once running, GKE clusters
are secure by default, highly

00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:17.250
available, monitored, and
they run on Google Cloud's

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:18.630
high-speed network.

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:22.410
They can also be fine tuned for
zonal and regional locations

00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:24.330
and can use specific
machine types

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:27.470
with optional GPUs or TPUs.

00:01:27.470 --> 00:01:30.690
GKE clusters also offer
hassle-free operations,

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:33.640
with auto scaling,
auto-repair of failing nodes,

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:35.310
and an auto-upgrade
to the latest

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:37.440
stable version of Kubernetes.

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:40.830
GKE is also a key
component of Anthos,

00:01:40.830 --> 00:01:44.070
Google Cloud's enterprise
hybrid and multi-cloud platform.

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:47.190
With Anthos, you can also
migrate existing VMs directly

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:50.070
into containers and move
your workloads freely

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:54.000
between on-premises and cloud
environments, such as GCP.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:57.330
What if you could focus on
building your stateless app,

00:01:57.330 --> 00:02:00.030
not on writing YAML
files, and still deliver

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:02.100
code packaged in a container?

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:05.490
The second way you can deploy
your containers on Google Cloud

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:07.320
is with Cloud Run.

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:10.860
This gives you the benefits of
both containers and serverless.

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:13.410
There is no cluster or
infrastructure to provision

00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:14.190
or manage.

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:16.770
And Cloud Run
automatically scales any

00:02:16.770 --> 00:02:18.450
of your stateless containers.

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:21.510
Creating a Cloud Run
service with your container

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:25.380
only requires selecting a
location, giving it a name,

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:27.750
and setting authentication
requirements.

00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:31.590
Cloud Run supports multiple
requests per container.

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:35.400
And it works with any language,
any library, any binary,

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:37.410
and even any base Docker image.

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:41.100
The result is service
with true pay-for-usage,

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:44.220
the ability to scale to
zero, and full out-of-the-box

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:47.040
monitoring, logging,
and error reporting.

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:50.130
Because Cloud Run is built
using the Knative open source

00:02:50.130 --> 00:02:53.130
project, which offers a
serverless abstraction on top

00:02:53.130 --> 00:02:57.270
of Kubernetes, you can have your
own private hosting environment

00:02:57.270 --> 00:02:58.920
and deploy the
exact same container

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:04.230
workload on Cloud Run for
Anthos in GCP or on prem.

00:03:04.230 --> 00:03:07.020
The third option for
deploying your containers

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:11.220
is straight to Google
Compute Engine, or GCE.

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:14.430
That's right, you can leverage
your familiar virtual machine

00:03:14.430 --> 00:03:16.780
environment to run
your containers.

00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:19.590
This means using your
existing workflow and tools

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:22.680
without requiring your team
to ramp up on all things cloud

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:23.250
native.

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:25.470
When creating a GCE
virtual machine,

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:27.030
the container
section will let you

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:29.250
specify the image
you'd like to use,

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:32.070
as well as a few
important options.

00:03:32.070 --> 00:03:34.110
When you get to the
boot disk section,

00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:37.200
the suggested virtual
machine OS is something

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:40.890
called a Container-Optimized
OS, an operating system

00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:42.960
optimized for running
Docker containers

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:44.250
and maintained by Google.

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:47.220
This operating system image
comes with a Docker Runtime

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:49.980
pre-installed, thus enabling
you to bring up your Docker

00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:53.070
container at the same time you
create your virtual machine.

00:03:53.070 --> 00:03:54.780
But it also lacks
most of what you

00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:57.580
expect to find in a
typical Linux distribution,

00:03:57.580 --> 00:04:00.720
such as a package manager
and many other binaries.

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:02.580
This means a
lockdown environment

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:06.240
that ensures a smaller attack
surface, keeping your container

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:08.025
runtime as safe as possible.

00:04:08.025 --> 00:04:09.900
The great thing about
running your containers

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:11.790
on Compute Engine is
that you can still

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:15.960
create scalable services
using managed instance groups,

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:19.920
as they offer auto scaling,
auto healing, rolling updates,

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:22.350
multi-zone deployment,
and load balancing

00:04:22.350 --> 00:04:23.790
for the compute instances.

00:04:23.790 --> 00:04:26.290
Where do these container
images come from?

00:04:26.290 --> 00:04:27.610
Where do I store them?

00:04:27.610 --> 00:04:28.830
How do I version them?

00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:30.910
And how do I restrict
access to them?

00:04:30.910 --> 00:04:35.100
The answer lies in Google
Container Registry, or GCR,

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:37.800
which is a private-by-default
Container Registry that

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:40.570
runs on GCP with
consistent uptime

00:04:40.570 --> 00:04:42.420
and across multiple regions.

00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:45.120
You can push, pull,
and manage images

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:50.040
in GCR from any system, VM
instance, or your own hardware,

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:51.990
and maintain control
over who can access,

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:53.640
view, and download those images.

00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:56.220
Note, also, how you
can conveniently deploy

00:04:56.220 --> 00:04:58.650
to all three run times
we've discussed straight

00:04:58.650 --> 00:05:01.810
from Container Registry,
deploy to Cloud Run,

00:05:01.810 --> 00:05:04.440
to Container Engine,
and to Compute Engine.

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:07.260
Container Registry works with
popular continuous delivery

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:11.370
systems, such as Cloud
Build, Spinnaker, or Jenkins

00:05:11.370 --> 00:05:14.580
to automatically build
containers on code or tag

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:16.320
changes to repository.

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:19.050
Finally, Container
Analysis scans

00:05:19.050 --> 00:05:21.630
containing images
stored in the registry

00:05:21.630 --> 00:05:23.520
for known
vulnerabilities, and keeps

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:26.520
you informed so that you can
review and address issues

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:27.840
before deployment.

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:30.570
Google Cloud offers
you three solid ways

00:05:30.570 --> 00:05:34.650
to run your containers, ranging
from a fully managed Kubernetes

00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:37.320
environment to a truly
serverless platform.

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:39.180
Pick the solution that
works best for you,

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:42.120
and start deploying your
containerized workloads today.

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:44.670
Consider trying some free code
labs linked in the description

00:05:44.670 --> 00:05:47.970
below to explore these products,
and look forward to upcoming

00:05:47.970 --> 00:05:50.190
episodes and more overviews.

00:05:50.190 --> 00:05:53.040
If you like this video, please
like, subscribe, comment,

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:55.920
share, and look forward to
more GCP Essentials videos.

